    ("AU", "Politics", MARCUS,
     "Illawarra councils agree to share one regional waste plan",
     "Four councils will plan landfill, recycling and organics collection together for the first time.",
     [
         "Wollongong, Shellharbour, Kiama and Wingecarribee councils have agreed to write a single regional waste plan, covering landfill space, recycling contracts and food and garden collection until 2041.",
         "Until now each council has planned and tendered on its own. Officials say that has meant higher prices, and trucks from Kiama and Shellharbour crossing council boundaries to reach the same landfill west of Wollongong.",
         "## What changes",
         "Under the agreement, signed on 14 October, the four councils will run joint tenders for recycling and organics processing and share data on what households throw away. A committee of 8 councillors, 2 from each council, will oversee the plan. Each council keeps control of its own bin collections and its own fees.",
         "“Our residents do not care which council owns the truck,” said a Shellharbour councillor who chaired the negotiations. “They care that their recycling is actually recycled and that the bill does not double.”",
         "## Landfill pressure",
         "The region's main landfill is expected to reach capacity within 12 years at current rates.",
         "Diverting food and garden waste, which makes up about 45% of a typical household red bin, is the plan's main lever for extending that. Councils elsewhere in New South Wales that collect food scraps in green bins report household landfill waste falling by roughly a third in the first year. Kiama and Wingecarribee would follow under the regional plan, with kitchen caddies delivered to about 40,000 homes.",
         "## Next steps",
         "A draft plan will go on public exhibition in February. Residents will be asked about collection frequency, bulky waste pickups and whether to open more drop-off points for electronics and batteries, which cause dozens of truck fires across New South Wales each year when they end up in household bins.",
         "Councillors expect to adopt the final plan by June.",
     ]),
    ("AU", "Business", SAM,
     "Wollongong's café owners face a squeeze as milk and rent costs climb",
     "Small hospitality businesses say prices have risen faster than customers will accept.",
     [
         "Café owners across Wollongong say rising costs for milk, coffee beans and rent are squeezing margins that were already thin, and several in Crown Street and Keira Street have cut opening hours rather than raise prices again.",
         "A survey of 112 hospitality businesses by a local business chamber found that 58% had raised prices twice in the past year, and 26% had reduced staff hours.",
         "## The cost of a flat white",
         "Owners said wholesale milk had risen by about 15% in two years, while green coffee beans have become dearer after poor harvests in Brazil and Vietnam. Commercial rents in the busiest blocks have also climbed as leases come up for renewal.",
         "“A coffee that cost me 90 cents to make three years ago costs nearly $1.40 now,” said the owner of a café near the Crown Street Mall. “I can't keep putting the difference on the customer.”",
         "## Changing habits",
         "Customers are buying fewer extras. Pastry sales are down, and more people bring a reusable cup to collect a 50-cent discount.",
         "Weekend trade remains strong, helped by visitors driving down from Sydney, but weekday mornings have softened as office workers in the city centre spend 2 or 3 days a week at home. One owner in Keira Street said Monday takings were 30% below what they were in 2019, while Saturdays were up.",
         "## Looking for help",
         "The chamber has asked Wollongong City Council to review outdoor dining fees and to promote the city centre to visitors on weekdays. Councillors said they would consider waiving footpath dining fees from December to February.",
         "Economists said the pressures were common across Australia, and that small businesses with long leases in busy locations were the most exposed when costs rose faster than sales.",
     ]),
    ("AU", "Technology", SAM,
     "NSW libraries lend portable hotspots to households without internet",
     "A pilot in regional libraries lets borrowers take home a mobile internet device for two weeks.",
     [
         "Public libraries in 12 regional New South Wales towns have begun lending portable internet hotspots, letting households without a home connection borrow one for 14 days at a time, the same way they borrow books.",
         "The pilot started in July. By October, 9 of the 12 libraries had a waiting list, according to the state library service.",
         "## Who borrows them",
         "Librarians said the devices were most often borrowed by parents whose children need to finish schoolwork online, by older residents applying for Centrelink or Medicare services, and by job seekers.",
         "“We have people who have been coming in every day just to use our wifi,” said a librarian in Cooma. “Now they can do it at the kitchen table, at 9 o'clock at night if that's when they have time.”",
         "## How it works",
         "Each hotspot comes with 50 gigabytes of data, enough for a fortnight of schoolwork and video calls. Borrowers sign a one-page agreement.",
         "Devices are reset and checked when they come back. Of the 240 hotspots lent so far, 3 have not been returned.",
         "## Measuring the gap",
         "Surveys suggest that about 5% of households in regional areas have no home internet, usually because of cost, and many more rely on a phone plan with a small data allowance. In some towns covered by the pilot, the nearest free wifi outside the library is at a fast-food restaurant on the highway.",
         "Digital inclusion advocates welcomed the pilot but called it a stopgap, and urged cheaper broadband plans for low-income households.",
         "The library service will decide in March whether to extend the scheme to another 30 branches.",
     ]),
    ("AU", "Science", MARCUS,
     "Citizen scientists log record numbers of frogs after a wet winter",
     "Volunteers recorded calls from more species than in any previous count.",
     [
         "Volunteers in a national frog count recorded calls from 182 species this year, more than in any count since the survey began 8 years ago, after a wet winter left ponds and creeks full across much of eastern Australia.",
         "Participants use a phone app to record frog calls for a minute or two at a pond or creek. Museum researchers in Sydney then identify each species from the recording and map where it was heard.",
         "## Why frogs matter",
         "Frogs are sensitive to changes in water quality, which makes them useful indicators of the health of wetlands. Several Australian species have declined sharply since the 1980s, partly because of chytrid, a fungal disease.",
         "“Every recording is a data point we could never collect ourselves,” said a researcher who manages the count. “Thousands of people listening on the same nights give us a picture of the whole country.”",
         "## Good news, with caution",
         "Some species not heard in parts of their former range for years turned up again. The green and golden bell frog, for example, was recorded at 4 new sites around Wollongong and Port Kembla.",
         "Researchers cautioned that a single wet year can produce a burst of breeding that does not last.",
         "## Backyard ponds",
         "More than 60% of recordings came from suburban backyards, school grounds and parks. That shows small urban ponds can support frogs if they are kept free of fish and garden chemicals, and if there is some cover, such as reeds or low shrubs, along the edge.",
         "The organisers encourage people to keep recording through November, when many species call most often. Schools can join a classroom version of the count.",
     ]),
    ("AU", "Health", SAM,
     "Regional pharmacies begin treating minor conditions without a doctor's script",
     "Pharmacists can now prescribe for some common conditions after extra training.",
     [
         "Pharmacists in regional New South Wales can now treat uncomplicated urinary tract infections, mild eczema and a handful of other minor conditions without the patient first seeing a doctor, under rules that took effect on 1 October.",
         "They must complete about 20 hours of extra training and follow set guidelines, which include sending patients to a doctor when symptoms are severe, keep coming back or do not fit the usual pattern.",
         "## Easing the wait",
         "Supporters say the change will help in towns where it can take a week or more to get a GP appointment. Many regional pharmacies are open until 8pm and on Sundays.",
         "“A woman with a urinary infection should not have to wait four days in pain,” said a pharmacist in Bowral. “If it's straightforward, we can start treatment today and tell her when she needs to see the GP.”",
         "## Doctors' concerns",
         "Some doctors' groups have warned that conditions could be misdiagnosed and antibiotics overused.",
         "They want the results watched closely. The state health department said it would track every prescription, referral and patient outcome, and publish a review after 12 months. An earlier trial in about 800 pharmacies found that roughly 1 in 10 patients was referred on to a doctor rather than treated.",
         "## What patients need to know",
         "Patients are asked to bring a list of their current medicines. Consultations cost between $20 and $30 and are not covered by Medicare. With the patient's consent, the pharmacist sends a summary to their regular GP the same day.",
     ]),
    ("AU", "Environment", MARCUS,
     "Lake Illawarra seagrass returns as water quality improves",
     "Monitoring shows the meadows that shelter young fish have recovered across much of the lake.",
     [
         "Seagrass meadows in Lake Illawarra now cover about 40% more of the lake bed than in 2019, according to monitoring by the state environment department, as water quality has improved and fewer nutrients have washed in from surrounding suburbs.",
         "Seagrass shelters young fish and prawns and helps keep the water clear by trapping sediment.",
         "## Behind the recovery",
         "Scientists credited several changes: upgrades to stormwater drains in Warrawong and Albion Park, native planting along Mullet Creek and Macquarie Rivulet, and works at the lake entrance at Windang that improved tidal flushing.",
         "“The lake was murky and smelly for a long time,” said a resident of Primbee who has kayaked on it for 30 years. “Now on a calm morning you can see the bottom in places you never could.”",
         "## Fish and fishers",
         "Recreational fishers reported better catches of bream and flathead this year.",
         "Scientists said fish numbers swing widely from year to year, and that 2 good seasons were not yet a trend. A state fisheries survey in May counted juvenile fish at 18 sites around the lake, and will be repeated each autumn so that changes can be compared over a decade rather than a summer.",
         "## Keeping it clear",
         "Shellharbour and Wollongong councils have asked residents to keep fertiliser and grass clippings out of gutters, and to pick up after dogs near the shore. Nutrients from those sources feed the algae that smother seagrass.",
         "Monitoring will continue every 6 months, and results will be posted online.",
     ]),
